    23	## Part 1. The UTC upper bound
    24	
    25	### What Bitcoin consensus does and does not bound
    26	
    27	- **[F] Median-time-past (consensus).** A header is invalid if `nTime ≤ MTP(prev)`, where MTP is the median of the
    28	  previous 11 timestamps (Bitcoin Core `validation.cpp`, "time-too-old"; `chain.h` `nMedianTimeSpan = 11`). This
    29	  bounds a timestamp **below, relative to earlier timestamps**. It does not bound it relative to real time. A
    30	  miner may set any `nTime > MTP` however late the block is really mined.
    31	- **[F] Two-hour future rule (node-local, not strict consensus).** A node rejects a header with `nTime > now +
    32	  7200 s` (`MAX_FUTURE_BLOCK_TIME = 2*60*60`, result `BLOCK_TIME_FUTURE`). The block can be accepted later, once the
    33	  node's clock catches up. Since Bitcoin Core 27.0 (PR #28956), `now` is the node's own system clock, not
    34	  network-adjusted time. This bounds `nTime` **above** real time, so it gives a real-time **lower** bound
    35	  ("accepted no earlier than nTime − 2 h"), which V1 does not claim. It gives no upper bound.
    36	- **[F] Timewarp.** BIP 54 (status Complete) would require a period-start block to have `nTime ≥ nTime(prev) −
    37	  7200`. It is **not active on mainnet**; Core PR #35793 enables it only on regtest. Even if active, it is
    38	  header-relative and would not bound real time.
    39	- **[F] Consequence (already registered, A2.1).** Consensus gives no finite bound on how far a header time may lag
    40	  real time. A coalition with a majority of hash power can keep timestamps behind real time without limit, because
    41	  MTP only has to creep upward.
    42	- **[F] Observed honest behaviour (context only).** In a mempool.space sample of 481 blocks (heights
    43	  857514–858080, one observer), first-seen time minus header time ranged from −69 s to +82 s, median 41 s. The
    44	  sample is small and has one vantage point. It is not evidence of a bound.
    45	
    46	### U1: an explicit registered assumption on header times
    47	
    48	**Derivation.** Let h* be the selected height (A10.5). Let r(j) be the real (UTC) time block j was mined.
    49	1. **[F under registered assumptions]** `A_sig` existed before r(h*). Block h*'s merkle root commits to
    50	   `SHA256(A_sig)` through the OTS path (SHA-256 and OTS assumptions, A9).
    51	2. **[F under the hash assumption]** For j ≥ h*, r(h*) ≤ r(j), because block j commits to the hash of a chain
    52	   containing h*.
    53	3. **[A] A-UTC(Δ, m).** Among the m = 6 blocks h* … h*+5, at least one has `nTime(j) ≥ r(j) − Δ`, with Δ = 7200 s.
    54	   That is, its miner did not set its timestamp more than two hours before it actually mined the block.
    55	4. For that block j: r(h*) ≤ r(j) ≤ nTime(j) + Δ ≤ max over i in [h*, h*+5] of nTime(i) + Δ.
    56	
    57	**Result:** `A_sig`, and so the bytes and both signatures, existed before **U = max nTime(h* … h*+5) + 7200 s**. In
    58	the requested form, U = T + X, with **X = 7200 s + (max nTime(h* … h*+5) − T)**. The second term is computed from
    59	the receipt, typically about 50 minutes when blocks are honest. A stronger assumption, "block h* itself is honest",
    60	gives X = 7200 s exactly. **[R]** Do not adopt that stronger form: it puts the whole claim on one miner.
    61	
    62	**What is consensus and what is assumption.** Steps 1–2 rest on the cryptographic and chain assumptions that are
    63	already registered. Step 3 is new. It is not consensus, and nothing in the bundle checks it. Context **[E]**:
    64	suppose timestamps were set by a deliberately backdating coalition holding a share α of blocks, found
    65	independently. Then all six blocks are theirs with probability α⁶: about 7×10⁻⁴ at α = 0.3 and 1.6% at α = 0.5.
    66	Δ = 2 h is about 90 times the largest lag in the observed sample above. Δ mirrors the forward tolerance;
    67	choosing it is an author parameter decision.
    68	
    69	**Relation to A2.1 (no conflict).** A2.1 refuses to bound B − C, the service's *observation* lag, and keeps any
    70	SLA-style bound out of the pre-registration's assumption set. A-UTC bounds a different quantity: the lag between
