   114	## A10.3 Reorganization depth, at k = 6 and `DepthK = k − 1` — OWNER DECISION, NARROW-CLAIM (review A03)
   115	
   116	A9 excludes "Bitcoin history deeper than k" (:49-50) and calls "Reorganization deeper than k" a residual (C5,
   117	:144). A9 also imports k = 6 under `DepthK = k − 1` (:105) and A2.1's count, in which the including block is the
   118	first confirmation.
   119	
   120	**Definition.** The depth of a reorganization is the number of blocks it removes from the tip of the chain. A
   121	reorganization of depth d removes every block that has d or fewer confirmations when it occurs. At k = 6
   122	confirmations, five blocks follow the anchor block; P5c's `depth` counts those five (`depth = c − 1`, so
   123	`DepthK = 5`).
   124	
   125	**Correction.** The registered assumption is that anchors with k or more confirmations are permanent (A1.6:
   126	"anchors at depth ≥ k are treated as permanent"). P5c's `Reorg` action can orphan an anchor only while
   127	`depth < DepthK`, that is, at five or fewer confirmations. So a reorganization of depth exactly k, which removes an
   128	anchor with k confirmations, is outside both. A9's "deeper than k" read literally leaves that case inside the
   129	claim, with no evidence. The excluded residual is therefore **a reorganization of depth k or more**. This narrows
   130	A9's literal prose claim at equality. It changes neither the model nor its permanence assumption, and withdraws no
   131	checked model result. This does not affect C4's chain-order gap (`g + 5 < r`), which the review found correct. The
