   126	## Part 2. Capture visibility
   127	
   128	### The proposal, made exact
   129	
   130	**[F] Today.** A captor of both channel credentials (the DNS credential and the repository key, which the trust
   131	configuration pins) can publish a key assertion naming its own keys. V1 then accepts receipts under those keys (A10.4).
   132	
   133	**Mechanism.** §15.5 gains `service_keys`, an array of `{domain, kms_fp, gpg_fp, not_before, not_after, sources}`
   134	entries. A new authority check requires that the manifest's (`key_fp`, `sset[0]`) and the receipt's window (the
   135	DNS value, equal to the git value) **exactly equal** one entry for D. Three outcomes, by analogy with the pin rules
   136	(`HEADER_CONFLICTS_PIN` versus `HEADERS_NOT_COVERED`):
   137	
   138	| Receipt's keys and window | Verdict | Why it is stable |
   139	|---|---|---|
   140	| Equal to a listed entry | passes this check | entries are immutable |
   141	| Window overlaps a listed window, or starts at or before `as_of`, but is not listed | `INVALID / SERVICE_KEYS_NOT_LISTED` | windows never overlap and are never removed; completeness through `as_of` (below) |
   142	| Window starts after `as_of` | `UNVERIFIABLE / TRUST_HORIZON` | a later release may list it (allowed: A9.5, an earlier UNVERIFIABLE may improve) |
   143	
   144	**Release-checker invariants** (added to §5): entries are immutable and never removed; windows for D never overlap
   145	(this matches A9.6's sequential rule); **completeness**: every window with `not_before ≤ as_of` is listed. A new
   146	entry with `not_before ≤` the previous `as_of` is therefore rejected. **Issuer invariant:** no receipt ships under
   147	keys that are not listed in a published release. With these invariants, no later release can turn either INVALID
   148	row into a pass, so verdict stability (A9.4) holds. **[F]** V1 registers one window only (A9.6). Its config lists
   149	exactly one entry, fixed at the freeze.
   150	
   151	**Observation scope.** The guarantee holds for *relying parties who run an authentic verifier release, signed by
   152	an uncaptured release key, whose trust configuration lists D's service keys*. Within that scope, a captor of both
   153	channel credentials who lacks the release key cannot make a receipt under keys of its choosing pass V1. Codex's
   154	"relying parties with previously trusted issuer keys" is this set. "Previously" means listed in their release.
   155	**[F, honest limits]** This is non-acceptance plus a contradiction verdict for parties in scope. It is not
   156	detection by the issuer, and it is not public visibility. For a captor to convince an in-scope party, it must
   157	obtain a release that lists its keys. Such a release is a release-key-signed artefact. It is publicly visible only
   158	if releases are published and fetched publicly.
   159	
   160	### Failure modes
   161	
   162	- **Release-key capture.** This is distribution compromise, already a stated boundary (A9). It is not a new
   163	  assumption: a release-key holder can already substitute KSKs, repository keys and pins. With the release key
   164	  and both channels, a captor can ship a release that lists its keys. A *targeted* release, given to one victim,
   165	  is invisible to everyone else. That residual is what a witnessed log addresses. **Precondition:** §14.2 item 1
   166	  (release-key custody independent of the DNS credential, the repository key and AWS) is OPEN, and YubiKey 2 is
   167	  not in hand. If independence cannot be supplied, this check protects nothing beyond the present boundary, and
   168	  the author must be told (§14.2 item 1's NARROW-MODEL route).
   169	- **Stale trust file, honest new-window receipt.** The result is `UNVERIFIABLE / TRUST_HORIZON`: a safe false
   170	  reject until the relying party updates. It cannot occur in V1, which has one window. After a registered rotation
   171	  it is the expected class, and relying-party text must say "update the verifier".
   172	- **Rotation cost (post-V1).** One release per window (at most 90 days), published before the window opens.
   173	  Relying parties must update. Releases are already the channel for pin updates, so this adds an obligation on
   174	  timing, not a new process.
   175	
   176	### Does this change a registered claim?
   177	
   178	Yes, it is a **BROADEN**. C4 gains a third authenticated source for the key set (the release). A9's adversary
   179	clause and A10.4 gain a protection: no new receipts under captor keys, within the scope above. One check, one
   180	reason code, one schema field and three release invariants are added. No signed byte, P8 encoding, `A_sig`
   181	vector or scenario-mapping verdict changes. Row b (reattribution) is already INVALID. Because it changes C4 and
   182	the adversary text, it needs an amendment. **[R]** Put it in A10 as one signing act, not a separate A11 (Codex
   183	point 5: one corrected description). **[F]** No formal model run supports the new property. Its evidence is
   184	the check's construction plus vectors, and it should be labelled as such.
   185	
   186	### Brief comparison: a witnessed append-only log
   187	
   188	Key assertions would go into a transparency log, and the verifier would require an inclusion proof under a tree
   189	head cosigned by k witnesses, whose keys the release pins. This gives what the proposal lacks: split-view
   190	resistance against a targeted release, and issuer detection through monitoring. It costs log and witness
   191	operators, witness keys and a 2106 longevity plan for them, bundled inclusion proofs, and a monitoring duty.
   192	**[E]** That is weeks of work, not days. **[R]** Record it as the candidate approach for the broader visibility
   193	objective, with no commitment (Codex point 4). Replace A10.4's "registers option (c)'s mechanism … as a named V2
   194	obligation".
