    30	## A2.0 What the model surfaced, and a correction registered with it
    31	
    32	The state machine did what pre-registering models is for: it made a prose
    33	ambiguity mechanically undeniable. The author ratifies the **strict**
    34	reading — restated below on a chain-visible observable so that the issuer
    35	and the verifier check the *same temporal conjuncts* (under the issuance
    36	policy's tolerances; A2.1).
    37	
    38	One correction from the ratification dialogue is registered alongside,
    39	because leaving it implicit would invite a misreading of what the strict
    40	rule protects. Consider the **abandoned-anchor artifact**: an issuance
    41	attempt whose anchor lands in an in-window block but buries slowly; the
    42	strict issuer discards and re-issues, yet the discarded transaction
    43	persists and typically confirms later, so a complete, chain-verifiable
    44	(receipt, anchor) pair exists that was never shipped. Checked against the
    45	registered properties: this artifact violates **none of them** — none of
    46	the **pre-Amendment-2 registered set**; A2.2 below changes exactly that
    47	for the **chain-late subclass** (`confirmed_at > declared + δ`), which
    48	it rejects. An operationally abandoned but **chain-valid** artifact
    49	(predicate holds; no timely eligibility latch, so the attempt was
    50	refused — possible under the A2.1 clock roles) is *not* rejected by
    51	A2.2; it is governed by the A2.1/A2.4 standing rules: cryptographically
    52	valid, evidentially admissible, no protocol standing. The
    53	anchor's security content is the block time alone — backdating is bounded
    54	because no forger can place bytes in a past block (P5), and P6's
    55	uncertainty window is bounded by `anchor_time` because signing precedes
    56	block inclusion. *When* depth k was reached enters neither argument. What
    57	the depth-k-within-δ rule actually protects is different: no shipped
    58	receipt can be orphaned by a tolerated reorganization (the P5c
    59	`NoShippedOrphan` invariant), and the issuer's evidence trail stays free
    60	of receipts whose completion needs explaining. The residue the artifact
    61	does create — two verifiable receipts for the same content with different
    62	declared times — is **not closed by this amendment** (correction,
    63	2026-07-21: an earlier draft claimed closure via A2.2 and A2.4; that
    64	argument silently assumed the fused clock, under which every discarded
    65	attempt is chain-late. Under the ruled clock roles of A2.1 a discarded
    66	attempt can be chain-valid, A2.2 rejects only late-burial artifacts, and
    67	A2.4 confines standing without deduplicating content). Closure — a
    68	lineage/equivocation-evidence mechanism or an explicit dedup rule — is
    69	registered as an Amendment 3 obligation.
    70	
    71	The ratified form strengthens P5 with a new verifier-side conjunct and
    72	adds obligations; under A1.2's change discipline that requires this
    73	amendment. No A1.2 property is weakened; the A1.1 falsifier is not
    74	triggered (stated precisely in A2.6).
