     1	# Tessera — Phase 0 Pre-Registration, Amendment 10 — six wording corrections to Amendment 9, found by the plain-language pilot (2026-10-16)
     2	
     3	> **Status: PROPOSED. This document is the instrument and is not yet signed. The author's first commit containing
     4	> this file is its signing act** (the house form since Amendment 5). Drafted 2026-10-16 by the AI collaborator
     5	> (Claude, Opus 5.5, owner instance) from `docs/reviews/plain-pilot/CODEX-REVIEW.md` ("Findings about A9 itself",
     6	> A01–A06), A9, A8 §A8.3 and §A8.5, A2 §A2.1, A1 §A1.6, `docs/V1-PROFILE.md` §7.1–§7.2 and
     7	> `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md` §7. Revised 2026-10-16 after the Codex non-author review of this draft
     8	> (`docs/reviews/amendment-10/CODEX-REVIEW.md`), applying all eight findings as disposed in
     9	> `docs/reviews/amendment-10/DISPOSITION.md`. Layered on the original and Amendments 1–9. Per amend-don't-rewrite,
    10	> none of them is edited: every registered sentence this amendment changes is listed in §A10.7 with its source line
    11	> and its replacement, and is left standing in its file. **A10.4 is AWAITING AUTHOR RULING.** The author rules on
    12	> it in his own words before the signing commit; if he rejects or changes it, the owner revises A10.4 first.
    13	
    14	## In plain words
    15	
    16	This amendment corrects six wording problems in Amendment 9. It adds no mechanism. The verifier uses the same
    17	checks and gives the same verdicts. A review of the plain version of Amendment 9 found the six problems.
    18	
    19	Bitcoin is a public system that adds blocks to a chain. A block header states a chain time. This value is not a
    20	measurement of real time. The registered number k is six.
    21	
    22	- **A01.** Amendment 9 says that the content existed "no later than" T. T is a chain time, not real time. The
    23	  correction says this in the claim and in the other sentences that use T in this way.
    24	- **A02.** Amendment 9 says, in its own words, that the keys "were allowed to issue during a stated window". The
    25	  verifier makes only two comparisons with the window. The correction states only those two comparisons.
    26	- **A03.** Amendment 9 excludes Bitcoin history "deeper than k". The correction defines the depth of a
    27	  reorganization. The exclusion then also includes a reorganization of exactly k blocks. The models already
    28	  exclude that case.
    29	- **A04.** Amendment 9 does not say which receipts V1 protects after an attacker obtains authority-record
    30	  credentials. The author must decide this item.
    31	  - The recommended text protects the frames and signatures that the service keys made honestly. This protection
    32	    applies before and after the capture.
    33	  - V1 does not prevent new receipts under the attacker's own keys.
    34	  - The service must publish its authority record. A third party can inspect a substituted key if that record is
    35	    public. The verifier does not check publication. V1 does not guarantee that an attacker publishes the record.
    36	- **A05.** Amendment 9 does not give the order of the steps that select a Bitcoin attestation. The correction
    37	  gives the steps that the profile already uses.
    38	- **A06.** DNS (the Domain Name System) holds one authority record. Amendment 9 says "owner name D". The verifier
    39	  must check the DNS owner name `_tessera-v1.wamason.com`. The correction names it.
    40	
    41	Each correction has a kind:
    42	
    43	- CLARIFY: the meaning does not change.
    44	- NARROW-CLAIM: the claim becomes smaller, so that it states only what the checks establish.
    45	- CORRECT: the wording disagreed with a registered rule or model. The correction follows that rule or model
    46	  without reducing the claim.
    47	
    48	---
    49	
    50	**Provenance labels** (as in A9). **AUTHOR DECISION**: the author ruled, in his own quoted words, dated. **OWNER
    51	DECISION**: taken by the owner instance under the project ownership the author assigned on 2026-10-12; not an
    52	author ruling. **RECOMMENDED**: drafted, not decided by anyone. What the signature means for OWNER items is as in
    53	A9: the author has read them and does not object.
    54	
    55	**Weakening (A1 §A1.1).** No A1.2 property, registered check, verdict, reason code, parameter (δ, ε, k, N, S),
    56	vector, encoding, model, or scenario-mapping verdict, fixture or safe-decision category changes. A10.1–A10.4
    57	narrow sentences of A9's prose claim to what A9's own checks, A8.3's runs and the cited models establish; no
    58	evidence is withdrawn. A10.3 removes one boundary case from the claim that the registered assumption (A1.6) and the
    59	model (P5c) never covered. This signed amendment is the record A1 §A1.1 requires for each narrowing.
    60	
    61	---
    62	
    63	## A10.1 "Existed no later than T" is chain time, not UTC — OWNER DECISION, NARROW-CLAIM (review A01)
    64	
    65	A9 says the bytes and signatures "existed no later than a time T fixed by Bitcoin" (A9:36-37; again at :114,
    66	:133 and in C5, :144). A9 also says that header times are chain-time proxies (C5) and that a finite UTC cutoff
    67	"requires an additional, explicit backward-skew assumption that V1 does not establish" (:444-446). The ordinary
    68	reading of "no later than T" is a real-time bound, which A9 does not support.
    69	
    70	**Reading adopted.** "No later than T" means chain order: the bytes and exact signatures existed before the
    71	selected Bitcoin block was mined, because that block commits to their SHA-256 hash (under the registered
    72	cryptographic and chain assumptions). T is that block's header time, a chain-time value. It is not an upper bound
    73	in UTC: A2.1 registers no finite bound on B − C, and neither ε nor S is one. A UTC reading needs the additional
